WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.340
The Dumbo live-action trailer just dropped
with a gorgeous look at Ninth Wonder of the

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.280
Universe, the world's only flying elephant.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.330
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm breaking down the first teaser

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.390
trailer for Dumbo which is jam-packed with
easter eggs and other intriguing details you

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:17.689
might have missed.

00:00:17.689 --> 00:00:21.800
The trailer kicks off with a cover of Baby
Mine, the beautiful and iconic song that Dumbo's

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.130
mother sang to him while she was locked up
in solitary confinement.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.980
The song received an Oscar nomination for
best original song in 1942 and for the live-action

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.160
version it's sung by Norwegian singer-songwriter
Aurora.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.850
The song is part of a heart-breaking moment
from the original movie where Dumbo's mother

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.390
cradles the baby elephant in her trunk as
she sings the lullaby.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.770
At the time, Mrs Jumbo had been locked away
after getting angry when her son was tormented

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.110
by some boys.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:50.000
She was branded a Mad Elephant and there's
a nod to that in the "Mad Elephant" sign you

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.109
can see behind Dumbo in this image.

00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:56.429
And, just before that, it even looks like
another elephant was being carted away in

00:00:56.429 --> 00:00:58.350
a cage, perhaps his mother?

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.489
In the original, Dumbo's mother is still with
him at the end, so the trailer for the live-action

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.760
movie could be teasing something darker possibly,
akin to Tim Burton's previous movies.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.750
Now, in the original film, on the advice of
the crows, Timothy Q. Mouse employs a psychological

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.420
trick with a pretend "magic feather" to give
Dumbo the confidence that he can fly.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.280
The new movie tips its hat to that moment
when new character Milly holds the feather

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.520
up to Dumbo telling him to show others what
he can do.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:29.170
Indeed, Milly's words to the young elephant
echo Timothy Q Mouse's line when Dumbo flies

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.970
for the first time at the circus.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.030
"Let's show 'em, Dumbo."

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.070
"Here Dumbo.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.100
Show 'em."

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.100
By the way, the new film isn't going to be
a direct recreation of the original movie.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.220
In fact, at CinemaCon, Disney revealed that
while "the original ends with the elephant

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.630
flying", Tim Burton's film actually "[takes]
that as a jumping off point for Dumbo's newest

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.890
story" which will be an expanded version of
what we're expecting.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:54.340
Which means that the feather in this scene
here is probably more of a reminder to Dumbo

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.340
of what he can do already, rather than a mental
trick to get him to consciously fly for the

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.910
first time in front of an audience.

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:05.140
I think Milly is going to play a key role
in the new movie – in fact, notice the actual

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.320
key she's wearing as a necklace.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.420
And she seems to be replacing the part of
Timothy Q. Mouse as Dumbo's best friend and

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.420
confidante.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.620
She's played by Nico Parker, the daughter
of actress Thandie Newton and filmmaker Ol

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.620
Parker.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.280
I love this image of a pink elephant surrounded
by bubbles, which is a lovely call-back to

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.920
the scene in Disney's classic animation where
Dumbo and Timothy Q Mouse accidentally got

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.250
drunk on champagne and started hallucinating
pink and multi-coloured elephants!

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:35.860
Also, notice, as he's about to be raised up
on this platform here, that Dumbo is wearing

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.170
a triangular-shaped headpiece with a D on
it, which very much reminds me of the scene

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.590
in the animated movie where Dumbo carries
a flag adorned with the letter D as he's about

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:48.740
to launch himself into the air to form the
top of an elephant pyramid.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:53.490
Speaking of pyramids, the choreography and
geometric shapes in this scene here pay homage

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.240
to the extravagant musical numbers staged
by famed director and musical choreographer

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.710
Busby Berkeley, which is actually 100% perfect
for Tim Burton's Dumbo because, as well as

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:07.480
films such as 42nd Street, Busby Berkeley
also choreographed the musical Billy Rose's

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.319
Jumbo, which just happens to be another movie
about a circus featuring an elephant named

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:17.320
Jumbo, and, of course, Jumbo Jr was Dumbo's
real name in the original animation.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.540
It looks like Burton is going to wow us with
plenty of fantastic visuals of Dreamland and

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.560
I'm curious whether that "Wonders of Science"
attraction might be a sly reference to the

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.030
former Wonders of Life pavilion at Epcot,
Walt Disney World.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.510
Something it doesn't look like we'll see adapted
from the animation are the storks which deliver

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.530
the baby circus animals including Dumbo himself.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:41.470
There's likely a nod to this via the flock
of birds which fly past the elephant's enclosure,

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.180
which seems like a bit of a message to the
audience that we shouldn't expect exactly

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.610
the same movie as the original because no,
these aren't storks.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:53.100
Another thing I really don't think we'll be
seeing in this new film are the animated crow

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.459
characters as they've been heavily criticised
as racist stereotypes of African Americans.

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:02.290
They do, of course, sing the famous "When
I See An Elephant Fly" song and I wonder if

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.010
we'll get an updated version of that in this
movie.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:09.660
As I mentioned before, the new movie is a
sort of continuation or expansion of the original

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:14.930
film from 1941, by the end of which Dumbo
was famous, with aircraft even being made

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.340
in his likeness during World War Two.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.650
In terms of the timeline of the live-action
film, that's set just after the Second World

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:22.650
War.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.960
Notice that Colin Farrell's character Holt,
who's a veteran, is missing an arm, presumably

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.270
an injury from the war; and he and his two
children discover Dumbo for the first time

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.569
hiding underneath a pile of straw.

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:37.230
At Disney's CinemaCon presentation, it was
revealed that Danny DeVito runs a travelling

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.819
circus that's struggling to keep going.

00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:44.620
DeVito's character is called Max Medici and
he's trying to use Dumbo's fame as an "amazing

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.050
flying elephant" to bring people to the circus.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:51.059
However, Medici also has to reprimand his
employees for their mistreatment of Dumbo

00:04:51.059 --> 00:04:54.789
and it seems this might be why Dumbo's hiding
away here.

00:04:54.789 --> 00:04:58.729
I think the scene where Holt and his kids
discover Dumbo might also be a little tribute

00:04:58.729 --> 00:05:03.349
to when Mrs Jumbo unwrapped her new delivery
from the storks in the animated feature and

00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:06.759
revealed the adorable calf for the first time.

00:05:06.759 --> 00:05:10.360
And talking of Dumbo and his mother, I also
liked how the weather vane at the beginning

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.529
of the trailer has a young elephant holding
onto the tail of an older elephant, just like

00:05:14.529 --> 00:05:18.139
we saw Dumbo do with his mother many times
in the original film.

00:05:18.139 --> 00:05:21.389
Now, how excited are you for the new Dumbo
movie?

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:25.249
And what other cool details or easter eggs
did you spot in the trailer?

